.Dd May 27, 2026
.Dt ORACLED 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm oracled
.Nd Oracle capability-world service manager
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl d
.Op Fl p Ar pidfile
.Op Fl T
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the userspace service manager for the Oracle capability world.
It is started by
.Xr rc 8
and manages access to the
.Xr cap_rt 4
capability runtime.
.Pp
During startup,
.Nm
executes the following phases:
.Bl -enum
.It
Daemonize, write pidfile, open syslog.
.It
Harden the process via
.Xr procctl 2
(subtree reaper, OOM protection, trace disable, signal-exit logging).
.It
Open
.Pa /dev/cap_rt
and claim it via the isolation service so that only
.Nm
can access the device.
Foreign processes receive
.Er EACCES
from the MACF hook when attempting to open it.
.It
Shield itself via the capprotect service (block ptrace, ktrace,
wait4, scheduler manipulation; hide from
.Xr ps 1
and
.Xr top 1
in production mode).
.It
Create a control socket at
.Pa /var/run/oracled.sock
for administrative commands.
.It
Enter the event loop.
.El
.Pp
Administrative commands are sent via
.Xr oraclectl 8 .
The control socket supports shutdown, reload, kernel module
loading and unloading, and system reboot.
.Pp
The control socket is interim infrastructure.
The long-term plan replaces it with
.Xr cap_rt 4
pair channels when
.Nm
becomes the system init.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width "-p pidfile"
.It Fl d
Run in the foreground and log to standard error as well as syslog.
In this mode, the capprotect shield omits
.Dv CP_SF_VISIBLE
and
.Dv CP_SF_CORE
to ease debugging.
.It Fl p Ar pidfile
Write the process ID to
.Ar pidfile
instead of
.Pa /var/run/oracled.pid .
.It Fl T
Test mode.
Run without requiring root, without daemonizing, and without
applying privileged process controls, capability runtime setup,
or the control socket.
Intended for automated tests.
.El
.Sh PROCESS HARDENING
When not running in test mode,
.Nm
uses
.Xr procctl 2
to:
.Bl -bullet
.It
Become a subtree reaper for future child processes.
.It
Mark itself protected from OOM kills.
.It
Disable tracing and core dumps.
.It
Enable kernel logging for fatal signal exits.
.El
.Pp
Additionally,
.Nm
activates a capprotect shield via the
.Xr cap_rt 4
MACF policy.
The shield blocks ptrace attach, ktrace, wait4 status scraping,
and scheduler manipulation from processes outside the
.Nm
fork family.
In production mode (the default), the shield also hides
.Nm
from process enumeration and suppresses core dumps.
.Sh DEVICE ISOLATION
.Nm
claims
.Pa /dev/cap_rt
through the cap_rt isolation service.
Once claimed, only processes sharing the
.Nm
nonce (its fork family) can open the device.
All other processes, including root, receive
.Er EACCES .
.Pp
The claim is held by the isolation service instance fd.
If
.Nm
exits or crashes, the kernel closes the fd and releases the
claim automatically.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/var/run/oracled.sock"
.It Pa /var/run/oracled.pid
Default pidfile.
.It Pa /var/run/oracled.sock
Control socket.
Accepts commands from
.Xr oraclectl 8 .
Mode 0700 (root only).
.It Pa /dev/cap_rt
CAP_RT control device.
Isolated by
.Nm
at startup.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr kqueue 2 ,
.Xr procctl 2 ,
.Xr cap_rt 4 ,
.Xr oraclectl 8 ,
.Xr rc 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared in 5BSD as the initial Oracle userspace service manager.
